I had the following error, poured through the newsgroups and the web,
asked for help on a mailing list, and finally was able to find the
solution.  I saw some posts regarding this error so I thought I'd post
my solution in the hopes it would help others.  The error message I
received during high network load was:

eth0: Transmit timed out: status 0050  0c00 at 473220/473248 command
000c0000

I have a DS20E running alpha Redhat 7.1.  The steps for success may
need to be modified slightly if you are running a different
distribution or a different kernel version (Redhat 7.1 has kernel
2.2.17).

1) Download pci_scan.c and kern_compat.h from
http://www.scyld.com/network/updates.html
2) Download eepro100.c from
http://www.zelow.no/floppyfw/download/modules/old/patched-2.2.17/
3) Put them in some directory on the computer (I use /usr/src/modules)
4) Make sure you have the source for your current kernel in
/usr/src/linux
5) Follow the compile commands at the bottom of the two .c files,
except
   a) make sure to use the gcc compiler provided by Redhat, not kgcc
(yes it is a controversial compiler but the module won't compile with
kgcc)
   b) make sure to add the -I/usr/src/linux/include option onto each
compile command
6) Copy the resulting pci_scan.o and eepro100.o into
/lib/modules/<your kernel>/net, making sure to back up the old ones
just in case of a problem (but don't leave the backed up ones in the
same directory as they will be included by depmod the next time module
dependencies are calculated)
7) /etc/init.d/network stop (stop networking)
8) rmmod -r eepro100 (remove loaded module eepro100)
9) depmod -a (calculate new dependencies)
10) modprobe eepro100 (insert module)
11) /etc/init.d/network start (start networking)

Hope that helps anyone with the same ethernet problems on alpha Linux.


