Nothing is wrong with your system, and it behaves as I would expect it
to under all cases except the last (2.4.17 with no mem=), when it crashes.

> > P00>>>sho mem
> >
> > Slot    Type       MB     Base
> > ----    ----      ----    --------
> >  0      DIMM       256    0
> >  1      DIMM       256    20000000
> >  2      DIMM       256    40000000
> >  3      DIMM       256    60000000
> >  4      DIMM       256    80000000
> >  5      DIMM       256    a0000000
> >  6      DIMM       256    c0000000
> >  7      DIMM       256    e0000000
> >
> > Total             2048

Notice the mapping; each 256MB DIMM is located in the bottom half of a
512MB area. Thus, of the total 4GB (32-bit) memory address space, only
half is actually populated, ie there are "holes" between each of the
DIMMs' address spaces.

For example, slot #0's 256MB DIMM has an address space of 0 to 0fffffff,
while the total slot #0 address space is 0 to 1fffffff.

When using the "mem=2048M" argument, you are reducing the *total* address
space to 2GB, into which only *half* of the actual memory falls, because
of the "holes", thus the usable memory is 1GB.

> > Cropping memory from 3840MB to 2048MB

This is done automatically by the 2.2 kernels, because their drivers are
not capable (on Alpha) of addressing more than 2GB of address space (whether
that space is full of memory OR NOT, as is your case).

> > Linux version 2.4.17 (root@bw138isd) (gcc version 3.0.3) #1 SMP Sat Feb
23
