> > memcluster 4, usage 0, start   196608, end   229376
> > memcluster 5, usage 0, start   262144, end   294912
> > memcluster 6, usage 0, start   327680, end   360448
> > memcluster 7, usage 0, start   393216, end   425984
> > memcluster 8, usage 0, start   458752, end   491515
> > memcluster 9, usage 1, start   491515, end   491520

The memclusters describe the actuall memory present in the system; you
can see the "holes" here quite clearly.

> > setup: forcing memory size to 2097152K (from 3932120K).
>......
> > Memory: 1008632k/2097152k available (1646k kernel code, 37896k reserved,
> 352k data, 296k init)

Notice that the space is 2GB but the memory only fills half.

> > Linux version 2.4.17 (root@bw138isd) (gcc version 3.0.3) #1 SMP Sat Feb
23
> 18:16:01 CET 2002
>............
> > Memory: 2028480k/3932120k available (1646k kernel code, 66584k reserved,
> 352k data, 296k init)

Here we see nearly all the address space (the code doesn't count the
empty space beyond the last DIMM), and all of the actual memory.

> > DAC960: ***** DAC960 RAID Driver Version 2.4.11 of 11 October 2001 *****
> > DAC960: Copyright 1998-2001 by Leonard N. Zubkoff <lnz@dandelion.com>
> > [4.2]
> > Unable to handle kernel paging request at virtual address
000000000000002c
> > CPU 0 swapper(0): Oops 0
> > pc = [<fffffc00008fc830>]  ra = [<fffffc00008fc5ec>]  ps = 0007    Not
> tainted

Uh, oh.

Assuming that the above pc= is somewhere in the DAC960 driver, I think
you've found a limitation in that driver, ie that it won't work
correctly with more than 2GB of memory address space on Alpha. I just
looked at the sources, and it is using the old "virt_to_bus()" routine
for getting the bus address of the DMA memory, which, on Alpha, uses
the "direct map" technique and is limited to 2GB of address
space. Since your memory occupies nearly 4GB of address space (see
above :-), it is probably attempting to use memory that is located at
an address above the limit.

The driver should be using "pci_map_xx()" and "pci_unmap_xx()"
routines, which are fully capable of handling as much memory as one
can put into an Alpha. This is NOT simply a matter of replacing the
virt_to_bus() calls with calls to pci_map_single(), as one also needs
to release any resources (via pci_unmap_single()) that may have been
allocated by the matching pci_map_single() calls.

Solutions:

1. fix the driver
2. run on your AS1200 with memory space limited to 2G

Good luck.

