It is an often-discussed point in this group that changing the 
graphics (or any other) card may require you to load a newer 
console firmware version - use Google to search.

First check whether anybody posted some "will never work" 
regarding that card in either this group or in "comp.unix.tru64".

If there is no such posting, I would suggest to get and install 
the newest version of SRM. Again, this is described in several 
postings, use Google for details.


> [...]  pitty enough both Tru64 and Linux run via SRM. Installing any of 
                                     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
I can not tell about the 2100A, but on other models Linux can 
also run via ARC, so this does not hold true in general.

> them without the card beeing seen by SRM will install in textmode, without
> CDE, because there is no X-server and there will never be as long as SRM
> does not report a graphic card upon installation.
> NT and W2K have no problem and accept any card, be it ISA, EISA, or PCI,
> because they use the ARC console.

If you had read here about a week ago, you would have read my posting 
that ARC also will not work with arbitrary graphics cards but only 
with the ones it knows how to handle, so (in this regard) there seems 
to be no difference between SRM and ARC. 
(See the thread "Graphic Card" less than two weeks ago in this group, 
esp. my posting of Feb 11.)


