Further to my previous mails i shall give a very clear practical example.
2100A with Qvision Eisa card, officially installed with the Configuration
utility and visible in the SRM console. 5 Harddisks on the Mylex DAC960
configured as JBOD, which gives me 5 independent disks (DRA0 - 5). Installed
Tru64 5.1 on DRA0 and this runs perfect, including CDE and all graphic
functions. Installed W2K on DRA1 and, apart from degrading the card to 800 x
600 as standard VGA, because of missing drivers, Windows has no problems.
Now i moved the Qvision one slot up and booted Tru64 with following results:
Graphics gone, no CDE, running in textmode only and complaints about missing
Xserver when trying to call CDE. Card is not visible in SRM. Booted W2K
again: no difference, graphics like before. Tried several different cards
from Eisa via AT to PCI. All were accepted by Windows and - if a matching
driver was available - tied in with full resolution. I put the Qvision back
in (in the wrong slot) and ran the Configuration utility, which recognized
it automatically and saved this to the nvram. After booting Tru64, all
graphic functions were 100% back again. Booting Windows no problem.
This makes it very clear, that Tru64 does not scan the hardware, but just
takes the updated information from the nvram (and that is what you see with
"Show dev"), whereas Windows does what all their versions do: Scan the
hardware directly and check for matching drivers, or replacements
(degraded). Because of this, i also have a simple Soundblaster PCI card
running under Win, but never recognized by SRM and Unix.
The only restriction with graphic cards in a 2100 is the fact that not all
can handle the busspeed and the picture collapses when the console is
starting. Generally said: when there is a picture in console mode, Windows
can run, but Tru64 can only run CDE when the card is visible in SRM.

Greetings
Harry




