	It'll be highly dependent on your software.  Theoretically the
500 MHz 21164 is a 1 GFlop machine, and I have extracted that performance
from it.  On a molecular-orbitals calculation, a 500a takes 13.25 secs
when compiled with Digital Visual Fortran 5.0; the same problem with a
Digital Visual Fortran 5.0 D executable took 9.3 secs on a Celeron
1 GHz laptop.  So, perhaps a 750-800 MHz x86 would be comparable.  It
might be worthwhile getting a cache chip fitted if it doesn't already
have one.  From memory that was worth around 10-20%, but again that
will be very software dependent.  Ah, google to the rescue:

