Yes, the last comment is very usefull. It's funny how you (the
original poster) can describe the same problem I'm having so
correctly. I have found a solution which might help you

I assume you use a SCSI system.

Installing RedHat is fairlly easy if you use the Avanti Milo, this
milo recognises SCSI devices. So you can boot the cd quite easely :

boot sr0:/kernels/generic.gz root=/dev/sr0 load_ramdisk=1

I tried debian this way, but debian doesn't have a noncompressed shell
on the cd, so you always get an init=   error.

So installing debian is best done from floppies, you need 3 of 'm, a
Milo, a rescue disk and a root disk.

When using the XL milo I get the same problem  you get, but I found a
way to work around that. Connect an IDE device to your alpha
containing a FAT partition. After loading milo switch to the fat
partition, then place the rescue floppy in the drive and type :
boot fd0:

there is a milo.cfg on the disk which contains all the config you need
for installing Debian, so there is no need to pass on parameters

I don't know why, but now the XL does read your floppy and you'll be
able to install debian from cd.

If anyone of you has got a milo that does work with the floppy drive,
I would like to know, maybe you can mail me the image or point me to
it. I would appreciate it very much

But as described in the text I qouted, It's very usefull having a
(small) FAT partition on your SCSI drive containing :
-linload.exe,
-milo,
-kernel,
so you don't need a floppy for booting anymore

good luck, Quido

BTW can you use a root.bin located on a cd for the ramdisk? there is a
root.bin in the /boot dir on the debian cd, so a floppyless install
would be possible on a XL

