Tom Crabtree <tomc@frontieranalytical.com> wrote:
> What kind of HD do you have?

A controller type could be more interesting.

> If it's an IDE drive SRM will probably not recognize it and therefore not
> boot.

All current versions of SRM for not-so-old boards actually boot from IDE
drives and ATAPI CDs but this may not hold for older hardware.  If your
SRM is obsolete then maybe there is an update for it?  Quite often this is
the case.  Can you boot from a floppy (dva0)?

It is rather imposible to boot from something which is not visible
to SRM.  I know of cases when actually adding in an old IDE drive
for a sole purpose of beeing a boot device was used as a solution
when a SCSI controller was not supported by SRM.  Once Linux boots it
will find a hardware which was not necessarily visible to SRM and your
root file system does not have to be on a boot device.

  Michal


