On Wed, 06 Feb 2002 10:12:15 +0100, Joerg Bruehe <joerg@sql.de> wrote:
>...
>To overcome this porting problem, the Compaq C compiler and linker 
>have an option "-taso" (= "address short option"), resulting in 
>all addresses being less than 2 (or 4?) GB so they fit into an "int". 
>The compiler is available for Alpha-Linux as well, but I do not 
>know whether the options works in that version as well; 
>AFAIK there is no such option in the GNU "gcc".

On my RH7.1 Alpha Linux system, I don't see the -taso switch in
either the man pages or the output of "gcc -help" or "ld
--help".  However, I _have_ used this switch on RH6.1 or RH7.0.
I think it was on RH6.1 that I had to add the following to
CFLAGS:

        -taso -Wl,-taso

The plain "-taso" _should_ be all that is needed on modern
systems.  IIRC, the "-Wl,-taso" was required for RH6.1 but not
for RH7.0, because the RH6.1 edition of gcc did not pass the
-taso flag to ld automagically.

Other things useful in porting 32-bit apps to Alpha linux include
the following:

        #include <linux/personality.h>.

        At the start of main(), add a call,
        personality(PER_LINUX_32BIT);

With these three items, and a few hacks to the poorly-designed
32-bit-only code, I was able to get Squeak (a free implementation
of Smalltalk from a bunch of former and/or current Apple fans)
working on RH6.1 Alpha Linux.  If ever there was a 32-bit-only
application, Squeak is one.

